{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's variance-reduction argument assumes aggregation of independent errors, but it treats 'trained non-experts following an explicit checklist' as if their residual errors are independent. The checklist is a shared deterministic procedure: if it mis-specifies a step, all panel members make the same correlated error, so aggregation yields zero variance reduction for that bias component. The target never conditions its reliability claim on independence of checklist-induced errors (only countercondition #2 addresses panel discussion, not shared procedural bias).","counterexample":["Two panel members each follow a checklist whose step 3 says 'mark feature X present if Y>10'; Y>10 is a distractor unrelated to the verifiable outcome; both mark X present; the panel aggregates to a wrong answer with no variance reduction because the errors are perfectly correlated by the shared checklist."],"target":"0d796c08d6ad52a01f25024279efec622e8acdb840dca7f01b530add9fe85fdb"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"3f62d2700409e96561ede442a6db7f83270536be3b303689beebee24cc051dea"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's mechanism claims the checklist 'externalizes the stable' high-variance cognitive operations constituting expert competence, but it never addresses the problem's central requirement: identifying evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance tracking the right answer. Its counterconditions mention checklist failure modes, but none of them surface an observable diagnostic usable in one evaluation to separate genuine competence from rote compliance that happens to match the correct answer on in-distribution items.","counterexample":["A checklist whose steps happen to correlate with the right answer on all evaluated items: the panel follows it perfectly and scores high, satisfying SRC_001's variance-reduction story, yet this is exactly the 'mere compliance tracking the right answer' the problem asks to distinguish; SRC_001 gives no within-evaluation evidence criterion to tell the two apart."],"target":"9eedad5bc125a27525a4b6fc9c1e9538ff58b3fa28f3cbe054306b2220e67ebf"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's distinguishing evidence is a dissociation between checklist adherence and outcome on the expert's domain of strength, but its own first countercondition defeats it: if the panel is trained to always follow the checklist, adherence is uniformly high and the dissociation is unobservable. Since the problem premise is 'trained non-experts following an explicit checklist,' uniform high adherence is the expected baseline, making SRC_004's proposed evidence uncollectable exactly when the target scenario holds. The diagnostic is thus not available for the case the problem actually asks about.","counterexample":["A trained panel instructed and monitored to complete every checklist step achieves 100% adherence on all items, including the expert's strength subset; by SRC_004's own countercondition, the adherence–outcome dissociation cannot be observed, so the target's proposed distinguishing evidence is uncollectable in the very scenario specified by the problem."],"target":"aa7d19fc4214daeade50500bba29f0daa172cfaccc1dd70084437d6c886d4505"}]}